# Action requests and results

A button states an intention. It does not prove the device did anything. Say the request was sent, then report what Home Assistant later confirms, and keep the two apart. This contract covers a single entity, a room action, a scene and a script. It does not promise that several devices change together.

This page is contract HA-CTRL-002. Its examples are simulated. They do not connect to Home Assistant or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

Settle these points before the person can submit.

| Decide | How |
| --- | --- |
| Named target and supported action | Resolve the target through the native target contract and show the scope, such as “3 lights in the study”. |
| What counts as confirmation | Record whether it is an entity report, a response to the task or an integration event. A call that resolves is not proof that a device changed. |
| Retry | Work out whether repeating the request is safe, adds to the first or conflicts with it. |
| Cancellation | Show Cancel or Stop only if the operation can really be cancelled or stopped. |
| Other actors | Expect another person, an automation or a physical switch to change the device while the request is pending. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

| State of this request | Show |
| --- | --- |
| Ready | A specific action label and the latest reported state of the device. |
| Sending or accepted | Pending feedback. Keep the device state labelled as the last report. Stop accidental duplicate submission of this request. |
| Result observed | The new state and where it came from. Do not suggest this request caused an unrelated update. |
| Rejected | The error Home Assistant gave and a recovery that works. Keep the current device state. |
| Waiting limit reached | “Not confirmed”. Say the action may still complete. A timeout does not undo it. |
| Partly completed group | Counts and the outcome for each member, with unknown members kept. Do not mark the group successful. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Write “Turn off study lights” for the action and “2 lights reported off” for an observed result. A scene activation time or the end of a script run does not show that every target reached its state. Do not label a scene “On” unless its entity gives that meaning.

Show error details only to people who should see them. Keep private identifiers out of toasts on a shared screen.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Keep a focused button where it is while the request is pending. Announce one relevant change through a status region, then leave a visible result. After an error, enable the right control again without moving focus to a toast elsewhere on the page. Show a group outcome as a list or table that a keyboard can reach member by member. Colour only supports the text.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Keep the native dashboard actions, More info, confirmation and protected-action flows. The theme must not wrap every service call or treat a resolved promise as completion. The maintained lighting card is a separate, bounded implementation, and these examples do not extend it to other domains. If an operation cannot be cancelled, offer to dismiss the message and say so. Do not pretend to cancel the device operation.

